    summary: "Scottish minimum unit pricing reduced alcohol-specific deaths by roughly 13%; cancer effects lag by 10-20 years. Propose a pre-registered long-term evaluation using registry data and synthetic controls (England), plus modelling to guide other jurisdictions.",
    hypothesis: "Minimum unit pricing reduces incidence of alcohol-attributable cancers (oral, oesophageal, liver, breast, colorectal) by at least 5% in the most deprived quintile within 15 years.",
    rationale: "Heavy drinkers are price-sensitive and alcohol harm follows a steep deprivation gradient.",
